course: dictionary
lesson: 8454
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word sail can be a noun, a thing, or a verb, an action. Context tells you which meaning the writer wants. «The Greeks sailed in triremes, fast warships with three rows of oars.» Sailed is a verb here: the Greeks travelled on water in ships.
explain: «The ball sailed over the fence.» A ball has no sail, so the meaning must be different. Sailed here means moved smoothly and fast through the air. Always ask: does this meaning make sense in this sentence? If not, try another meaning.
explain: Sail can also be a noun: a big piece of cloth that catches the wind. Look at the other words. «The ancient ships had huge white sails» tells you a sail is something a boat has, so here sail is a thing, not an action.
explain: Some meanings are only found in phrases. «To set sail» means to start a journey by sea. In a story, «It was plain sailing after that» means it was easy. You cannot work these out word by word, so when a phrase seems strange, read the whole sentence.
fact: A sail is a big piece of cloth on a boat that catches the wind and pushes the boat along.
fact: To sail is to travel on water in a boat or ship, or to control a sailing boat.
fact: To sail can also mean to move smoothly and fast, as in «The ball sailed over the fence».
fact: To set sail means to start a journey by sea.
fact: Wind is a force that pushes the sail, and windmills also have sails.
exercise: «The ancient ships had huge white sails.» What is a sail? | A sailor's cabin below deck | A small wooden oar used to row | A type of fish found in the sea | *A big piece of cloth on a boat that catches the wind
exercise: «The Greeks sailed in triremes, fast warships with three rows of oars.» What does sailed mean here? | To sleep under a cloth | To move through the air | *To travel on water in a ship | To sing a song
exercise: «The ball sailed over the fence.» What does sailed mean? | To control a boat | *To move smoothly and fast | To rest on the grass | To travel on water
test: «To set sail from Lisbon, the captain gave the order.» What does set sail mean? | To count the crew | To stop the boat at the harbour | *To start a journey by sea | To paint the ship white
test: In a story, «It was plain sailing after that.» What does plain sailing mean? | It was a long swim | It was cold and wet | It was very windy | *It was easy
exam: Tom says the word sail in «The boat's sail was torn» and in «We sail at dawn» is the same word with the same meaning. What is the difference? | Both are nouns for a boat | *The first is a noun for a piece of cloth, the second is a verb meaning to travel by boat | Both are verbs meaning to move fast | The first is a verb, the second is a noun for a ship
